// ---- Adaptive use of the parallel collector (a program's request, not ABLA_GC_WORKERS) ----------------------------------------
// The parallel mark and sweep beat the serial ones on a heap whose graph divides well (JSON documents, the compiler's, the editor's) and lose
// on others: a binary tree of a million nodes marks no faster on four threads (the page directory answers every pointer, each claim is a
// compare-and-swap) and a program of many small collections pays the threads' start and join each time. A program that asked for threads
// (runtimeMemorySetGcWorkers) gets them where they pay:
//  - a collection of fewer than 262,144 registry slots is serial (its pause is a few milliseconds; the threads cost about as much);
//  - otherwise parallel, unless parallel has been measured worse. The collector times each larger collection per 1,024 registry slots (page
//    directory, mark and sweep) and, when the modes of two consecutive collections differ, takes the ratio parallel / serial (consecutive ones, so
//    the heap has barely grown: the cost per slot rises with the heap); an average of the ratios decides, parallel when it is under 0.9 or unknown.
//    The other mode is tried (a probe) at the 2nd larger collection and then every 4th, 8th, 16th, ... up to 128th for as long as
//    a probe leaves the choice as it was (4 again when it changed it).
// ABLA_GC_WORKERS=n in the environment, and stress runs, always use the threads.
// State, static: +0 the previous larger collection's cost, +8 its mode (1 parallel, 2 serial; 0 none), +16 the ratio parallel / serial in
// thousandths (0 unknown), +24 larger collections since the last probe, +32 collections between probes, +40 1 while the mode in use is a probe,
// +48 the preferred mode before the probe (1 parallel).
private trusted fun __ablaRuntimeHostGcAdaptiveState(): int = nativeStaticAllocate(56)

/// True when ABLA_GC_WORKERS was set at start: collections then always use the worker threads, never the adaptive choice.
